  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nA 500 mb shortwave trough will round a 597 dm ridge over the SW \nCONUS on Wednesday as a sfc cold front approaches from the north \nWednesday night into Thursday. Initially, an influx of moisture and \nisentropic lift associated with a warm front tonight may trigger \nelevated showers and isolated thunderstorms mainly over SEMO \nWednesday morning. While the HRRR focuses elevated convection in\nthe morning, the NSSL/FV3 show more robust sfc based storms in \nthe afternoon and evening where SPC has a marginal/slight risk \nof severe weather. DCAPE of 900 to 1200 J/kg combined with \n7.0-8.0 C/km low-level lapse rates will pose a risk for \ndamaging winds. The overall thermodynamics are certainly \nsupportive with 2500 to 3000 J/kg of MLCAPE and 30-40 kts of \neffective bulk shear. However, the severe risk is highly\nconditional, as it is contingent on the morning convection that\nwould result in cloud debris. Humidity levels will also be on \nthe increase with dewpoints rising to near 70 degrees. \n\nAs the aformentioned cold front approaches Wednesday night, a \nscattered broken line of showers and storms tries to develop.\nRain pushes east into early Thursday morning across the entire \nFA. Severe weather chances likely wane overnight with the loss \nof diurnal heating, but additional development is possible \nThursday afternoon over the Kentucky Pennyrile as the front \nwashes out to the north. At any given point, rain chances peak \nat a 30-50% chance due to the large spread among the CAMs, making\nit difficult to pinpoint timing. Where storms occur, up to a \nhalf to one inch of rainfall will be possible, especially over \nsoutheast Missouri and western Kentucky where confidence is \nhigher in seeing some rain.\n\nWhile brief relief in the humidity arrives on Friday, a 594 dm \nridge of high pressure builds over the FA this weekend into \nearly next week that will allow for 850 mb temps to rise above \n20C. High temperatures are progged by Sunday and Monday to \nreach the low to mid 90s; however, dewpoints struggle to reach \n70 degrees due to less evaporational transport from agriculture.\nThis translates to heat index values only around 100 degrees. \nGiven that model ensembles show the ridge persisting through \nmuch of next week, would not be surprised to see temperatures \nreach the upper 90s at some point. Unlike the previous heatwave,\nthe lower dewpoints favor this being a much drier heat that \nwill make heat headlines unlikely through at least the weekend.",
